* Overview
This engine runs /this/ site. I write posts and project notes as `.norg` files, the app parses them at startup, and every request reads from memory. You get one binary, no database, and no JavaScript framework.

** Why build one
Off-the-shelf tools gave me two of these three at best:
- Neorg as the source format, with no Markdown step
- a single deployable binary
- projects as folders, each holding its own devlogs

** Stack
@table
| Layer | Tool | Notes |
| - | - | - |
| Server | `net/http` | stdlib mux, longest-prefix routing |
| Templates | `a-h/templ` | typed, compiled to Go |
| Content | `internal/content/norg` | my own parser, about 1,000 lines in five files |
| Code blocks | Chroma | highlighting at parse time |
| Styles | Tailwind v3.4.17 | standalone binary, pinned by SHA-256, plus handwritten CSS |
| Logs | zerolog | JSON in production |
| Deploy | Docker on Railway | distroless image, also published to GHCR |
@end

** What works
*** DONE folder-based project loader with draft and duplicate-slug checks
*** DONE nested routes for project devlogs
*** DONE flat devlog list on each project page
*** DONE search with `blog` and `projects` filters
*** DONE RSS and sitemap entries for posts, projects, and devlogs
*** DONE link validation, request limits, and pinned build inputs
*** DONE parser split into its own package
*** DONE coffee menu front page with a brand view transition
*** TODO phrase matching in search
*** TODO stemming so /rebuild/ finds /rebuilt/
*** CANCELLED comments through a third-party widget

** Terms
$ index.norg: the required entry file in each project folder; it holds the project metadata.
$ devlog: any other `.norg` file in a project folder, listed below the project with its date and reading time.

The whole engine is about 2,400 lines of Go and 2,200 lines of tests. The devlogs below cover how it got here.
